risks.impact_description:
If PROS does not deliver a stable REST API (including final request/response payloads and endpoint) and complete RTPE validation in a timely manner:
1) Boomi cannot proceed with full integration build, mapping sheet finalization, and error-handling implementation, leaving approximately 40% of Boomi work blocked;
2) Conga–Boomi–PROS integration testing in QA will be delayed, pushing out the June 3 integration plan completion and threatening the June 22 SIT start date;
3) Release 1 and Release 2 sprints will experience additional spillover as stories dependent on the PROS API cannot be completed, increasing the Red status risk for Release 1;
4) Server errors may persist in lower environments, undermining confidence in deal guidance outputs and forcing rework once corrected data and API behavior are in place, which could jeopardize the August 14 (Release 1) and September 14 (Release 2) go-live dates.

If the additional middleware responsibilities (XML-to-CSV conversion and delta-to-full-snapshot transformation) are not designed and implemented in time:
1) The PROS→EPM price ingestion flow may not be ready for Day-1 manual operations or Day-2 automation, delaying Release 2 price management go-live (September 14, 2026);
2) Integration design for EDL–SAP–PROS and PROS–EPM may need late rework if alternative mechanisms are adopted under time pressure;
3) Boomi and middleware teams may face unplanned scope increases, causing sprint spillover and impacting other critical integration work (including Release 1 flows);
4) Misalignment between PROS XML outputs and EPM CSV expectations could lead to data quality issues and manual rework in EPM, reducing trust in the new pricing operating model.

If the remaining three optimization fields (e.g., add-on and AI/non-AI indicators) and outstanding analytics attributes are not delivered and validated on time, the team will:
1) Be unable to expand guidance generation beyond the initial ~50 attributes, limiting the usefulness of raw pricing guidance for business review;
2) Face delays in building and validating analytics dashboards (e.g., UDD charts requiring ACV%, discount% and related measures);
3) Experience spillover of data- and analytics-dependent user stories into subsequent sprints; and
4) Potentially compress SIT and UAT timelines, increasing the risk of defects or incomplete analytics functionality at go-live.

If the anticipated 4–6 Release 1 and 7–8 Release 2 stories spill over into the next sprint without adjustment:
1) Future sprints will have increased scope, raising the risk of further spillover and schedule slippage;
2) SIT (planned for 2026-06-22) and UAT (July 2026) windows may be compressed, reducing time available for defect resolution and stabilization;
3) The August 2026 Release 1 and September 2026 Release 2 go-live dates could be jeopardized if critical spillover stories relate to core optimization, price management, or analytics capabilities; and
4) Team capacity and morale may be impacted due to sustained high workload and frequent rollover of incomplete stories.

If Conga–Boomi field mappings, including customer and product identifiers, are not fully aligned and signed off by 2026-05-27, the project risks:
1) Rework of integration flows when inconsistencies are discovered during SIT or UAT;
2) Increased defects or incorrect pricing behavior due to mis-mapped fields;
3) Delays to the June 22 SIT start and subsequent test cycles; and
4) Potential slippage of the August 2026 Release 1 go-live date, as integration defects would need to be resolved and retested late in the schedule.

If the EPM–Semarchy migration does not complete by July 2026 or the SAP RPA decision is delayed or negative:
1) Automated or semi-automated processes for ingesting and managing price lists in EPM may not be ready, forcing continued manual operations beyond the planned Day-1 scope and increasing operational effort;
2) Integration and analytics work that depends on the target EPM/Semarchy stack could be delayed, compressing testing windows for Release 2 (September 2026 go-live);
3) A negative SAP RPA decision may require alternative approaches for SAP-side automation or manual workarounds, potentially increasing error risk and extending processing times; and
4) Overall program timelines and benefits realization could be impacted if these external platforms are not ready to support the intended pricing operating model.

If parent–child pricing cannot be fully automated in Release 1 and must rely on a manual one-time load and PROS’ native capabilities:
1) Enterprise customers with complex hierarchies may not consistently receive correct guidance when pricing rules are defined at the parent level and expected to cascade to child accounts;
2) Any changes to customer hierarchies after the one-time load may not be reflected in pricing guidance, leading to outdated or inaccurate prices;
3) Additional manual operational work will be required to maintain parent–child relationships and verify pricing outcomes, increasing the risk of error; and
4) Business stakeholders may lose confidence in early optimization outcomes for segments relying heavily on parent–child pricing, potentially limiting adoption until Release 2 improvements are delivered.
